==English==

===Pronunciation===
*Rhymes: [[Rhymes:English:-uːvi|-uːvi]]

===Etymology 1===
See [[groove]]

====Adjective====
{{en-adj-er-y|groov}}

# of, pertaining to, or having [[grooves]]

===Etymology 2===
From the phrase ''[[in the groove]]'', ultimately from the grooves of an early phonograph record.

Very popular during the 1960s, finding its way into several song titles, such as Simon and Garfunkel's "The 59th Street Bridge Song (Feelin' Groovy)" and The Fifth Dimension's "Workin' on a Groovy Thing". Seldom used now except as a deliberate 1960s reference.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj-er-y|groov}}

# {{slang}} [[cool]], neat, interesting

====Alternate spelling====
"Groovey" (as in the early Simon and Garfunkel tune, "We've Got a Groovey Thing Goin'").

